Lab 50: Introduction to Shell Variables

Objectives
Understand the concept of shell variables.
Learn how to set and use local and global variables.
Learn how to utilize special shell variables, such as $?, $$, and $#.
Prerequisites
Basic knowledge of the command line interface and navigating through the terminal.
An installed shell environment like Bash, available on most UNIX-like systems.
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Working with Local Variables
Step 1: Creating a Local Variable
A local variable is recognized only in the shell session where it is created.
MYVAR="HelloWorld"
Explanation: The command above sets the local variable MYVAR to the string "HelloWorld".
Step 2: Accessing a Local Variable
echo $MYVAR
Explanation: The echo command prints the value stored in MYVAR. Expected output is HelloWorld.
Task 2: Working with Global Variables
Step 1: Creating an Exported Global Variable
A global variable is available to all subprocesses or shell sessions.
export MYVAR2="GlobalHello"
Explanation: The export command makes the MYVAR2 variable available globally.
Step 2: Accessing a Global Variable in a Subshell
bash -c 'echo $MYVAR2'
Explanation: A new shell session is started using bash, which checks and prints the value of MYVAR2.
Task 3: Understanding Special Variables
Step 1: Using the $? Variable
Purpose: $? gives the exit status of the last executed command.
echo $?
Explanation: After running a command, use echo $? to see if it was successful (0 means 'success').
Step 2: Using the $$ Variable
Purpose: $$ represents the process ID (PID) of the current shell instance.
echo $$
Explanation: This command returns the PID, which is useful for scripting and process management.
Step 3: Using the $# Variable
Purpose: $# indicates the number of arguments supplied to a script or function.
bash -c 'echo $#'
Explanation: Run this script in an environment with no parameters to see a return of 0.
Conclusion
At the end of this lab, you should have a fundamental understanding of shell variables, both local and global. You learned to identify and use special shell variables, which provide useful insight into your shell environment and help in the effective scripting of utility tasks. Practice these commands regularly to enhance your proficiency in shell scripting.
